# Sample .htaccess cache-control directives for hexmob.win / dev.hexmob.win
#
# Apply this content to .htaccess in the web root on the server.
# It is NOT deployed automatically — configure once per server/hosting account. The deploy's
# mirror explicitly excludes .ht* precisely so it can never overwrite this file.
#
# Why the entry point is not "index.html":
#   The deploy renames Vite's index.html to index.php before uploading (see AGENTS.md §7).
#   This host's nginx open_file_cache serves stale static .html for days and cannot be flushed
#   without shell access; PHP-FPM revalidates from disk instead. A rule matching "\.html$"
#   therefore never applies to a deployed site — the file does not exist under that name.
#
# Two classes of file, two policies:
#   - Hashed build output (e.g. assets/index-C7h0xmLY.js): the content hash is in the filename,
#     so a new build produces new names. Cache for a year, immutable.
#   - Entry point (index.php): must be revalidated on every load, or visitors stay pinned to a
#     bundle that no longer exists after a deploy.
# Everything else (public/ images with stable names, seo/ cards) is deliberately left to the
# host's defaults: those filenames do not change when their content does, so a long TTL would
# keep serving replaced bytes. Cache them only if you accept that.

<IfModule mod_headers.c>
    # Hashed build output — filename changes with content, safe to cache forever
    <FilesMatch "-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{8}\.(js|css|woff2?|ttf|eot|png|jpg|jpeg|svg|gif|ico|webp)$">
        Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
    </FilesMatch>

    # Entry point, served through PHP-FPM
    <FilesMatch "^index\.php$">
        Header set Cache-Control "no-cache, must-revalidate"
    </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

# What NOT to add:
#   - a rule for "\.html$" — nothing is served under that name any more (index.html is deleted
#     by the deploy so a stale copy cannot be picked up by the directory index);
#   - a long TTL for non-hashed files (public/ images, seo/ social cards) unless you accept that
#     replacing such a file on the server keeps visitors on the old bytes;
#   - anything that requires a module the shared host may not have enabled — keep the blocks
#     inside <IfModule> so a missing module degrades to "no headers" rather than a 500.
